## Find each probability from its angle
A full turn is $360^\circ$. The lower half is $180^\circ$ and the 60° and 120° sectors fill the upper half.
P(\text{win } X) &= \frac{60}{360}
&= \frac{1}{6}
P(\text{win } 19) &= \frac{120}{360}
&= \frac{1}{3}
P(\text{lose } 20) &= \frac{180}{360}
&= \frac{1}{2}
## Set the expected value equal to 0
Expected value is the sum of each points value times its probability. Losing 20 points counts as $-20$.
19 \times \tfrac{1}{3} + (-20) \times \tfrac{1}{2} + X \times \tfrac{1}{6} &= 0
\tfrac{19}{3} - 10 + \tfrac{X}{6} &= 0
## Multiply every term by 6 and solve
38 - 60 + X &= 0
X - 22 &= 0
X &= 22
ANSWER: X = 22

## Measure the two lengths with a ruler
Place the ruler along [AB] and read off the length in cm.
|AB| &= 4 \text{ cm}
Place the ruler along [EF] and read off the length in cm.
|EF| &= 10 \text{ cm}
ANSWER: |AB| = 4 \text{ cm}, \quad |EF| = 10 \text{ cm}

## Find the sugar in the 2021 recipe
$30\%$ less sugar means $70\%$ of the original.
\text{Sugar in 2021} &= 35 \times 0.7
&= 24.5 \text{ g}
## Find the sugar in the 2025 recipe
A further $20\%$ reduction means $80\%$ of the 2021 amount.
\text{Sugar in 2025} &= 24.5 \times 0.8
&= 19.6 \text{ g per 330 ml}
## Scale up from 330 ml to 500 ml
There is $\frac{19.6}{330}$ g of sugar in each ml.
\text{Sugar in 500 ml} &= 500 \times \frac{19.6}{330}
&= 29.6969...
Round to 1 decimal place.
&= 29.7 \text{ g}
ANSWER: 29.7 \text{ g}
